1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,986
Hello and welcome.

2
00:00:01,986 --> 00:00:08,930
My name’s Heather McCallum and I’m one of the potters who work here at

3
00:00:08,930 --> 00:00:09,870
Edelman Pottery.

4
00:00:09,870 --> 00:00:14,723
Before we go into the workshop, I just want to say a bit about

5
00:00:14,723 --> 00:00:16,110
the craft of pottery.

6
00:00:16,110 --> 00:00:19,146
Then we’ll have a look at the equipment,

7
00:00:19,146 --> 00:00:22,520
and you can try making a pot of your own.

8
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:28,778
Like many people, I’m sure you know that pottery as an art form is

9
00:00:28,778 --> 00:00:31,281
tens of thousands of years old,

10
00:00:31,281 --> 00:00:35,870
and we know this because it stands the test of time.

11
00:00:35,870 --> 00:00:42,037
Things like baskets and pictures don’t survive in the earth in the same way

12
00:00:42,037 --> 00:00:43,270
that pots do.

13
00:00:43,270 --> 00:00:49,927
And even if ancient pots are found in small pieces, they still provide a

14
00:00:49,927 --> 00:00:52,780
lot of information about the past.

15
00:00:52,780 --> 00:01:01,224
There’s no doubt that pottery has given archaeologists a fascinating insight into how ancient

16
00:01:01,224 --> 00:01:02,350
humans lived.

17
00:01:02,350 --> 00:01:06,926
The shape of an artefact may have been lost,

18
00:01:06,926 --> 00:01:12,518
but archaeologists can tell whether the pots were for, say, storage

19
00:01:12,518 --> 00:01:19,128
or cooking by examining the impressions on the clay, the scratches from tools;

20
00:01:19,128 --> 00:01:24,720
and the clay itself can reveal where the pots came from.

21
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:30,278
When I ask people why they want to take a pottery class with me,

22
00:01:30,278 --> 00:01:32,660
they sometimes talk about these things.

23
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:39,912
Like our ancestors, they hope that something they create will also last longer than

24
00:01:39,912 --> 00:01:40,948
they do,

25
00:01:40,948 --> 00:01:47,164
that their work – whether it is good or not – might say something

26
00:01:47,164 --> 00:01:50,790
about humanity many years after their death.

27
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:55,550
Of course, you will all have your own reasons for coming here.

28
00:01:55,550 --> 00:02:02,015
As far as I’m concerned, what I love most is the concentration you need

29
00:02:02,015 --> 00:02:04,169
to make a good pot.

30
00:02:04,169 --> 00:02:08,910
That focus takes you away from the stresses of everyday life.

31
00:02:08,910 --> 00:02:16,002
If you’re elderly, it’s also good exercise for hands and wrists and helps with arthritis

32
00:02:16,002 --> 00:02:21,426
and, of course, it’s a fun activity for children because it’s

33
00:02:21,426 --> 00:02:22,260
so messy!

34
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:26,268
Here at Edelman Pottery, we show you some of the basic pottery techniques

35
00:02:26,268 --> 00:02:30,277
so that you can use these to create whatever you wish – a gift

36
00:02:30,277 --> 00:02:31,510
for a friend, perhaps.

37
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:38,566
Like nearly everyone who comes here, I’m sure this is the first time you

38
00:02:38,566 --> 00:02:40,917
will have tried the art,

39
00:02:40,917 --> 00:02:44,210
so we’ll keep things simple today.

40
00:02:44,210 --> 00:02:49,601
Now, before we move on, can I just say a word about what you’re

41
00:02:49,601 --> 00:02:49,960
wearing.

42
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:56,602
As we said in our email, please remove any watches, necklaces, etc.

43
00:02:56,602 --> 00:02:59,370
and put them somewhere safe.

44
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:03,963
If you have long hair, do tie it back now.

45
00:03:03,963 --> 00:03:06,260
We’ll provide aprons later,

46
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:12,231
but I trust your clothes are old but comfortable – not your favourite T-shirt

47
00:03:12,231 --> 00:03:13,150
or jeans!

48
00:03:13,150 --> 00:03:15,975
So now we’re in the workshop.

49
00:03:15,975 --> 00:03:20,010
Have a look around – there’s a lot going on.

50
00:03:20,010 --> 00:03:26,238
To make pottery that will last, you need a potter’s wheel, a kiln –

51
00:03:26,238 --> 00:03:31,576
which is basically a very hot oven where you fire the pottery –

52
00:03:31,576 --> 00:03:32,910
and some tools.

53
00:03:32,910 --> 00:03:34,714
So first, the kiln.

54
00:03:34,714 --> 00:03:41,030
If you look over in the far corner, you’ll see one of ours.

55
00:03:41,030 --> 00:03:47,190
Since their invention, kilns have changed very little, though in the past 20 years,

56
00:03:47,190 --> 00:03:51,590
a lot of progress has been made in temperature control.

57
00:03:51,590 --> 00:03:59,249
Basically, a kiln removes the water from clay at temperatures of around a thousand

58
00:03:59,249 --> 00:04:00,343
degrees Celsius.

59
00:04:00,343 --> 00:04:06,360
This allows anything you’ve made to set permanently in shape.

60
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:11,666
It’s a pretty ugly, heavy object that’s hard to keep in a house

61
00:04:11,666 --> 00:04:14,850
or flat so most people don’t have one.

62
00:04:14,850 --> 00:04:19,569
You may think, ‘Can’t I use my oven?’ Well,

63
00:04:19,569 --> 00:04:28,064
that’s possible but domestic ovens don’t really get hot enough and eventually the clay will crack

64
00:04:28,064 --> 00:04:29,480
and fall apart.

65
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:34,414
Some people fire pottery in a fire pit outside –

66
00:04:34,414 --> 00:04:38,800
but bear in mind that can be dangerous.

67
00:04:38,800 --> 00:04:45,816
You also need to know about safety procedures for kilns as they release toxic

68
00:04:45,816 --> 00:04:47,820
compounds into the air.

69
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:51,455
Every potter needs a potter’s wheel.

70
00:04:51,455 --> 00:04:58,724
This machine is used to shape the clay into an object with circular walls

71
00:04:58,724 --> 00:05:01,840
or sides – such as a bowl.

72
00:05:01,840 --> 00:05:10,632
Its invention revolutionised the pottery industry, allowing multiple items to be produced in a

73
00:05:10,632 --> 00:05:11,260
day.

74
00:05:11,260 --> 00:05:17,498
Lastly, there are a number of different tools that potters use, depending on what

75
00:05:17,498 --> 00:05:19,280
they want to make.

76
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:23,825
When you start, your hands can make all kinds of shapes

77
00:05:23,825 --> 00:05:27,130
and curves without relying on a sculpting tool.

78
00:05:27,130 --> 00:05:33,519
However, there are some basic tools that you will need to handle the clay

79
00:05:33,519 --> 00:05:34,888
on the wheel.

80
00:05:34,888 --> 00:05:41,277
Some look very strange and have even odder names that you may find hard

81
00:05:41,277 --> 00:05:42,190
to remember.

82
00:05:42,190 --> 00:05:48,209
Rather than go through them all now, I’ll just name a few tools as

83
00:05:48,209 --> 00:05:49,412
we go along.

84
00:05:49,412 --> 00:05:55,030
We can provide these and I wouldn’t recommend spending money on them yet.

85
00:05:55,030 --> 00:06:01,320
So let’s try making a pot of your own …
